            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 44-year-old female, admitted on 05/07/1985 with classic signs of acute appendicitis, including right lower quadrant pain, nausea, fever, and elevated inflammatory markers. Clinical examination and abdominal ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis. Following admission, the patient received intravenous fluids, analgesics, and prophylactic antibiotics. Vital signs were monitored continuously. A laparoscopic appendectomy was performed without intraoperative complications. The postoperative course was uneventful, with adequate pain control and wound healing. The patient was mobilized early and received discharge education regarding wound care and activity restrictions. She is discharged home in stable condition on 08/07/1985 and may resume normal activities gradually. Please arrange a follow-up appointment in approximately two weeks. Do not hesitate to contact me should any concerns arise.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
